<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="they-only-tickle-when-you-swallow">They Only Tickle When You Swallow</h2>

  <p>It was a normal beginning of a summer day in the kitchen of Loraine Wills at the Lake Cliff guest ranch. I had just restocked the woodbox, Grover McDaniels was trading barbs with "Lo...rain...y" as he called her in his Texas drawl, and Billy was cutting slabs from a cured ham he had just taken from a window cooler on the shaded side of the sprawling ranch house.</p>

  <p>Loraine had laid the ham slabs in a large cast iron skillet, poured Grover a cup of coffee and ordered me away from the stove. I usually obeyed her without question as she was a force to reckon with when she had her dander up, but as the skillet began to sizzle I saw the slabs come to life and I couldn't take my eyes from them. Little snow-white maggots, disturbed by the heat, were crawling over the upper sides.</p>

  <p>Refrigerators hadn't entered our lives and window coolers or spring sheds protected perishables, to a certain extent. Blow flies were a constant problem however, and in this case had found a hole in the screen.</p>

  <p>Placing a hand over my mouth Loraine commanded: "Billy, come here please." Grover was still struggling with his shoes as Loraine pointed at the skillet. Billy never at a loss for words said, "I'll have mine well done, but I expect Grover will want his squirming a little."</p>

  <p>As Billy held Grover's attention Loraine disposed of ham, occupants and skillet in the garbage pit. Later, Grover ate his scrambled eggs without a question about the ham he had smelled frying.</p>

  <p>In August of a later year I was at the Lane ranch when a couple of fellows came driving in with a small buck deer in the trunk of a 1937 Plymouth coupe. They were fishing in upper Shallow Creek when a small herd of deer crossed near them, Al always carried a pistol and when the buck appeared he took a quick shot at it's head. Wonder of wonders it dropped on the spot and spoiled their fishing trip.</p>

  <p>I helped them skin and quarter it. Then, after disposing of incriminating evidence, we hung the meat in a large root cellar. After two weeks of aging Art came from Creede and we carried the quarters to a cabin to finish carving and divide the free venison.</p>

  <p>It resembled the ham at Wills' ranch, only more so. Maggots were well entrenched in the ribs and neck with the hind quarters carrying fly blow. As we cleaned it and cut the worst of the rot from choice cuts Art warned me, "Damn you, don't you tell a soul about this or no one will eat it."</p>

  <p>Several days later I saw Al and he told me that was the best venison he had ever eaten and he planned on getting another next August. I couldn't vouch for the taste. I like wild meat, but I passed that well tenderized venison for potatoes and gravy.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Loraine and Billy Wills of the Lake Cliff Ranch appear in "Echoes from the Thundermug," in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/married-life-and-mining-camps-part-three/#black-drought-basics">"Black Drought Basics."</a> The "Lane ranch" belongs, per family knowledge, to the family of <a href="/people/anna-maud-woodruff/#charles-lane">Charles Lane</a>. "Art" here is given no surname and is not confirmed to be a specific person documented elsewhere on this site.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="smooth-as-silk-agoin-or-acomin">Smooth as Silk A'Goin' or A'Comin'</h2>

  <p>I returned from work one evening to find my bride all hunched over with a death grip on the sink. She looked as pale and shaky as Wylie Turnquist when he learned his girlfriend had flunked the rabbit test.</p>

  <p>I figured she had either met the Overnight Express driver, who travels like a bat out of hell on our narrow road, or she had lost a footrace with one of the whiteface bulls in the west 40.</p>

  <p>As it turned out it was far worse, in her estimation. She had been using more water than on a normal day and had gone up hill to check the depth of water in our culvert-type storage sump. There she found a mouse, hairless and bloated, floating in silent repose. She had spent the remainder of the day retching, choking, gagging and dragging herself around.</p>

  <p>In 1953, I left the Phoenix property to lease the Holy Moses group of claims from Allen Hosselkus, Alex Duncan and Ralph Ellithorpe. I installed a water tank in First Gulch with an outlet near the road in East Willow Creek Canyon. From the tank we drew drill water, compressor coolant and drinking water. It became less than potable after a couple of months, not putrid but with what could be called a distinctive taste and odor.</p>

  <p>William "Scooter" Jackson, who was helping me sink a winze in #2 Tunnel, had long since quit drinking from the overflow pipe as had the crew from the Outlet Mine. Pete Walker, our ore hauler, and I continued to drink our fill each day and filled our canteens from this icy cold, snow melt and remarked to each other that the pipe must be rusting to cause the peculiar taste.</p>

  <p>After we mucked out and drilled the floor of the winze Scooter would leave me to tamp explosives as he did maintenance work outside the portal. He usually hauled a barrel or two of water for the next day's use among other duties. On this day I had timed the fuses leading to the primers lit them with the flame of a carbide lamp and climbed ladders to reach tunnel level. I walked a safe distance from the hoist station to count the reports.</p>

  <p>Satisfied there had been no misfires I walked the additional half mile to sunlight and fresh air. I had just hung up my diggers when Scooter came in the dry room with a board in his hands. He handed it to me and I turned it to the light where I could see the barely identifiable remains of a mouse and chipmunk spread-eagled and beginning to smell.</p>

  <p>"I had time to clean the water tank," he advised me, "and here's what has been fouling up the water."</p>

  <p>I wasn't very proud of old Scooter as I lay stretched out on the waste dump trying to turn myself inside out.</p>

  <p>A friend once told me of the smoothness of a drink he had concocted, Old Crow whiskey and Cranapple Juice. I tried it, but as Pete Walker will attest, no drink approaches the smoothness of First Gulch ice water laced with mouse fur.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>This is a variant of "Smooth as a Biscuit" in "Echoes from the Thundermug," <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/grown-up-in-creede-part-three/#smooth-as-a-biscuit">told there</a> with differences kept exactly as printed in each: the Echoes version names Les Turnipseed in the opening comparison, and this one names a different man, Wylie Turnquist, in a different predicament, so the two are not the same story; Echoes says "UPS lady" where this book says "Overnight Express driver"; Echoes says "Thirty years ago" where this book gives the date as 1953 (Echoes' "Stealing Ain't As Easy As It Was In Time," in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/creede-boyhood-part-one/#stealing-aint-as-easy-as-it-was-in-time">Creede Boyhood, Part One</a>, says the Holy Moses lease came two years before a 1954 larceny, which would put the lease in 1952, a year off from the date given here); and Echoes names "the crew from the Phoenix Mine" where this book names "the crew from the Outlet Mine." This book alone names William "Scooter" Jackson, <a href="/people/william-charles-jackson/">John's brother</a>, in the story. Wylie Turnquist is not the same person as Newt Turnquist of Union Supply Company, who appears elsewhere in "Echoes from the Thundermug"; nothing ties the two Turnquists together. Allen "A.J." Hosselkus, Alex Duncan and Pete "Ralph" Walker are documented elsewhere on this site; Ralph Ellithorpe is named only here.</em></p>
